WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:01.751
Just a moment ago tonight, Elon Musk,

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:02.919
in an interview with Andrew Ross

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:03.962
Sorkin at The New York Times

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:04.796
DealBook summit,

00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:06.965
saying this to advertisers

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:08.091
who've left X over

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:12.053
his endorsement of anti-Semitic content.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:13.471
If somebody tried to blackmail me

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:16.474
with advertising, blackmail with money

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:20.687
yourself,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.230
but

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.233
go yourself.

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:27.277
Is that clear?

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:29.738
I hope it is.

00:00:29.738 --> 00:00:30.572
Seems pretty clear.

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:32.198
OUTFRONT now, Dana Hull, Bloomberg

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.117
senior reporter who covers Elon Musk.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:35.535
So, Dana, The New York Times

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.704
reporting that this anti-Semitic post

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:40.707
could possibly cost up to $75 million

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:43.835
in lost ad revenue this year.

00:00:43.835 --> 00:00:44.794
Meantime, Musk is warning

00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:45.879
that the advertising boycott

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:46.671
could kill the company.

00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:47.922
He's not happy about it.

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:50.550
It's fascinating for him to say that,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.009
of course,

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:54.721
based on why the boycott happened.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:55.597
Yeah, I mean, I think

00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:57.098
what you saw tonight at the DealBook

00:00:57.098 --> 00:00:58.808
summit was authentic musk.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:00.310
I mean, he's angry about it,

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.270
but he's not going to change his tune

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.022
and he's not going to change the way

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:04.689
he operates

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:05.940
just because advertisers

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.318
are leaving the platform

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.069
and, you know,

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:10.779
dropping F-bombs at a big summit

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:13.114
like DealBook is unusual for a CEO.

00:01:13.114 --> 00:01:14.491
But as we all know by now,

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:15.075
Elon Musk

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.701
is not your typical CEO

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.870
by any stretch of the imagination.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.706
No, certainly not that that is true.

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:21.956
You know,

00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:24.084
he also talked about his recent trip

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.418
to Israel, saying it was planned

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:26.669
before the fallout,

00:01:26.669 --> 00:01:28.046
that it wasn't an apology tour.

00:01:28.046 --> 00:01:30.173
He did apologize, though, for the Post

00:01:30.173 --> 00:01:31.091
saying that of all the things

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:31.716
he's written on it,

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:33.093
it was the most foolish thing

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.011
he'd ever said on the platform,

00:01:35.011 --> 00:01:35.470
likening it

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.805
to handing a loaded gun

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:38.598
to those who hate me

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:39.516
and arguably to those

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:40.391
who are anti-Semitic

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:41.476
and saying he was quite sorry.

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:43.561
It wasn't his intention.

00:01:43.561 --> 00:01:45.146
Is this just trying to save face

00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:48.108
or is that actually genuine?

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:49.484
You know, I think it's a mixture of both.

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:51.611
I mean, we finally get this sort of quasi

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:52.612
apology, what,

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.697
two weeks after the initial posts

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:56.574
that is still up on the platform.

00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:58.159
It has not been deleted.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:01:58.868
And, you know,

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:02.038
I think that Musk is not always the most

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:03.623
emotionally intelligent person.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:04.666
He's not always the best

00:02:04.666 --> 00:02:05.625
reader of the room.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.836
He tends to sort of shoot from the hip

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:10.088
and say things without a filter.

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:11.214
And that can backfire,

00:02:11.214 --> 00:02:12.215
which we've seen kind of

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:13.091
throughout his career.

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:14.175
But he you know,

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:15.051
this was the closest

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:15.760
that we've seen him

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.886
come to

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:18.555
expressing regret and remorse

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:19.639
for the way that his words

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:20.473
were construed.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:21.266
I mean,

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:22.851
you know, some advice would be

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:25.186
maybe not tweet or not post,

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:25.895
but he's obviously

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:26.980
the owner of the platform

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.064
and that's his prerogative.

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:28.481
I don't know that

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:29.649
that was going to get through,

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.150
especially because

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.818
of what we saw yesterday. Right.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:34.112
Supporting this Pizzagate

00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:36.865
long debunked conspiracy theory.

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:39.951
But could supporting that as well?

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:43.329
There are so many of these moments

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:45.456
when you add them all up.

00:02:45.456 --> 00:02:48.251
I mean, it does raise a question is

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:50.795
what is the future of X at this point,

00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:51.963
especially with advertisers

00:02:51.963 --> 00:02:52.505
are saying in the

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.382
numbers that we're seeing,

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:55.425
I, I think that

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:55.842
Musk is

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:56.342
really trying

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:58.386
to pivot to a subscription model

00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:00.555
where he wants creators and people

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:02.473
who, you know, want to be on X to pay

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:04.350
for different levels of access.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.727
And he does not want to be beholden

00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:06.686
to advertisers.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:07.812
And this is sort of a

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:10.481
I mean, the wild irony here is that,

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:11.566
you know, Musk created

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.776
Tesla, this incredible force

00:03:13.776 --> 00:03:14.986
in the automotive industry,

00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:17.113
without relying on advertising at all.

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:18.156
And then he bought

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:19.657
this social media company

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:21.910
that is heavily dependent on advertising.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.036
And I think that that's

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.996
sort of a circle that he can't square.

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:26.080
Yeah,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:26.915
listen, there is

00:03:26.915 --> 00:03:27.707
there's a lot there,

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:28.958
and I'm glad that you're watching it

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:29.626
so intently

00:03:29.626 --> 00:03:30.585
because you can translate

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:31.377
a lot of it for me.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.337
Danielle, thank you

00:03:32.337 --> 00:03:33.171
for being with us tonight.

